===Etymology 2===
1780, from {{etyl|non|en}} {{term|hrukka||wrinkle, crease|lang=non}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*hrunkijō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*hrunkitō||fold, wrinkle}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*(s)ker-||to turn, bend}}. Akin to {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|hrukka||wrinkle, crease, ruck|lang=is}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|runza||fold, wrinkle, crease|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Runzel||wrinkle|lang=de}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|ronse||frown|lang=dum}}. More at {{l|en|frounce}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To crease or fold.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To become folded.
#* '''1917''' ''"Will you come over now and try on your dress?" Ally asked, looking at her with wistful admiration. "I want to be sure the sleeves don't '''ruck''' up the same as they did yesterday."'' — Edith Wharton, ''Summer'', [http://etext.virginia.edu/etcbin/ot2www-pubeng?specfile=/texts/english/modeng/publicsearch/modengpub.o2w&act=surround&offset=727838576&tag=Wharton,+Edith,+1862-1937:+Summer;+a+novel,+1917&query=ruck&id=WhaSumm Chapter 12.]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A crease, a wrinkle, a pucker, as on fabric.

